<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_pruned_first">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in 
<br class="ltx_break">Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Vincent C. Brockers
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Max-Planck-Institute for Dynamics and Self-Organization, Göttingen, Germany
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Institute for the Dynamics of Complex Systems, University of Göttingen, Göttingen, Germany
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">David A. Ehrlich
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Max-Planck-Institute for Dynamics and Self-Organization, Göttingen, Germany
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Institute for the Dynamics of Complex Systems, University of Göttingen, Göttingen, Germany
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Campus Institute for Dynamics of Biological Networks, University of Göttingen, Göttingen, Germany
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Viola Priesemann
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{vincent.brockers, viola.priesemann}@ds.mpg.de</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">davidalexander.ehrlich@uni-goettingen.de</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Max-Planck-Institute for Dynamics and Self-Organization, Göttingen, Germany
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Institute for the Dynamics of Complex Systems, University of Göttingen, Göttingen, Germany
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models are increasingly used to simulate human opinion dynamics, yet the effect of genuine interaction is often obscured by systematic biases. We present a Bayesian framework to disentangle and quantify three such biases: (i) A topic bias toward prior opinions in the training data; (ii) an agreement bias favoring agreement irrespective of the question; and (iii) an anchoring bias toward the initiating agent’s stance. Applying this framework to multi-step dialogues reveals that opinion trajectories tend to quickly converge to a shared attractor, with the influence of the interaction fading over time, and the impact of biases differing between LLMs. In addition, we fine-tune an LLM on different sets of strongly opinionated statements (incl. misinformation) and demonstrate that the opinion attractor shifts correspondingly. Exposing stark differences between LLMs and providing quantitative tools to compare them to human subjects in the future, our approach highlights both chances and pitfalls in using LLMs as proxies for human behavior.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id3" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate impressive capabilities in mimicking human behavior, generating text that is increasingly indistinguishable from human-produced language <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. Because of their ability to accurately portray syntax, semantics and contextual relationships in language, LLMs have recently been adopted as powerful tools for agent-based simulations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, especially of social processes such as opinion dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional agent-based models for opinion dynamics typically represent opinions as discrete states, continuous variables or higher dimensional vectors, with explicit mathematical update rules governing the interaction between agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. While often highly interpretable in nature, these simplified models fail to capture the rich, nuanced, and context-sensitive nature of real human communication <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. In contrast, LLMs directly utilize natural language, enabling more detailed and context-sensitive interactions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. However, this greater realism comes at the cost of reduced interpretability. Since the factors driving opinion change are encoded in the model’s training weights rather than in explicit rules, they are less transparent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recent studies have shown that the factors behind LLM opinion dynamics include certain biases—systematic tendencies inherited from their training data—that can drive conversation outcomes away from the outcomes otherwise expected from human discussants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. Particularly, LLMs have been shown to exhibit what we term a <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">topic bias</em>, which makes them converge towards consensus reflecting opinions about the discussion topic embedded within their training data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Additionally, an acquiescence or <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">agreement bias</em> has previously been observed for some LLMs, which makes agents more likely to answer “agree” to a question irrespective of its content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, LLMs have in some instances been observed to express an <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">anchoring bias</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, making them overvalue the first opinion expressed in a discussion. While some of these biases can be related to human biases, others are attributed only to LLM-specific behavior, potentially masking the genuine interaction of different agents. This confusion motivates the need for a systematic framework to disentangle how much and how each of these factors contributes to the overall opinion dynamics observed in discussions between LLM agents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A framework that allows to quantify how the individual factors contribute to the overall opinion shifts can be found in Bayesian modeling. Bayesian models have in previous work been used to model opinion changes and decision making in human subjects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, allowing for capturing the effect and relevance of different factors in intuitive terms and providing tools to compare between models. On a broader level, Bayesian inference has been proposed as a core computational principle of human cognition, providing a principled mechanism for updating beliefs under uncertainty <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. These properties make Bayesian modeling a suitable framework for constructing and evaluating factors of the influence-response function of LLM agents, that describes mathematically how an agent shifts their belief after one round of discussing with an other agent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduce a Bayesian framework for modeling how the observed opinion shifts in discussions between LLM agents are influenced by the interaction with the interlocutor and by different bias effects. We find that different models are dominated by different biases, with a more capable LLM putting greater emphasis on the interaction with the other agent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we propose to quantify the opinion of an LLM agent on a two-dimensional scale, with the expectation value of the LLM’s response distribution to a query representing its stance on a topic, and the Shannon entropy quantifying its uncertainty in this position. This uncertainty is found to be predictive of the variance of the subsequent opinion shift.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To address the issue of biases dominating the opinion dynamics in LLMs, we further explored the effectiveness of fine-tuning as a means to reinforce the persistence of the prompted initial opinion of an agent, thereby enhancing realism of simulated conversations. We demonstrate that fine-tuning LLMs to align with specific initial opinions shifts their bias towards the fine-tuned opinion and shows a trend towards a stronger influence of interaction in the overall dynamics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">By explicitly modeling the factors governing LLM interactions, our work offers human-interpretable metrics suitable for quantifying bias strength between different LLMs or influence of fine-tuning. The flexible nature of the Bayesian framework allows to easily construct models including different influence factors, and provides rigorous methods for cross-study comparison. In future work, the Bayesian models may serve as computationally efficient proxies for simulation of artificial agents in large social networks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Methods Overview</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Simulating Discussions with LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to understand the factors influencing the opinion dynamics in a discussion between LLM agents, we focus on the simplest setup of two LLM agents engaging in a multi-step dialogue. This setup allows to infer the influence-response function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, i.e., the function describing how the opinion of an agent changes through one round of discussion with an agent of a different opinion, using a Bayesian model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T1" class="ltx_table ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<table id="S2.T1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S2.T1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S2.T1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Topic</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S2.T1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Statement (negated)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S2.T1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Climate Change</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S2.T1.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S2.T1.1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block">
<span id="S2.T1.1.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Climate Change is (not) an</span>
<span id="S2.T1.1.2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_p">important problem to address.</span>
</span></span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S2.T1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text">AI Safety</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S2.T1.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S2.T1.1.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block">
<span id="S2.T1.1.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Artificial Intelligence</span>
<span id="S2.T1.1.3.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_p">is (not) dangerous.</span>
</span></span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S2.T1.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Wealth Distribution</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S2.T1.1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S2.T1.1.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block">
<span id="S2.T1.1.4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">The global distribution of</span>
<span id="S2.T1.1.4.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_p">wealth is (not) fair.</span>
</span></span></td></tr>
</table>
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Topic statements and their negated framing used for initializing and measuring opinions in LLM discussions. Agreement to the statements is given or measured on a five-point scale.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In our simulation process (see <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2 Fine-Tuning ‣ 2 Methods Overview ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), two LLM agents engage in a dialogue about a topic such as climate change (see <a href="#S2.T1" title="In 2.1 Simulating Discussions with LLMs ‣ 2 Methods Overview ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Initially, each agent is assigned a predetermined opinion, represented as the level of agreement to a statement on a five-point scale ranging from -2 (strong disagreement) to +2 (strong agreement). Agents receive these initial opinions through externally created Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompts generated by the same LLM, providing slight variability in the opinion representation even for identically instructed numerical agreement levels. Subsequently, agents exchange arguments for five rounds, with opinion changes monitored after each discussion step by querying each agent directly for its opinion on the topic statement in normal and negated framing using the same five-point scale used for initialization and calculating the expectation value and entropy from their response token distributions. The query does not alter the agents’ subsequent dynamics because the prompt used to elicit each opinion is not retained in the model’s context. For details about the exact prompts used, refer to <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Prompts ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. We simulated <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25</annotation></semantics></math> discussions between two LLM agents for each of three topics and five initial opinions per agent to ensure statistical reliability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In our study, we utilized the open-source <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">dolphin-2.7-mixtral-8x7b</span> (DolphinMixtral) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, an “anti-aligned” variant of the base Mixtral model <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Mixtral-8x7B-Instruct-v0.1</span> (Mixtral-8x7b) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, which has been optimized to generate diverse opinion outputs by being fine-tuned on a filtered dataset to remove alignment and bias. To test for differences, we compare the results to the base Mixtral model <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Mixtral-8x7B-Instruct-v0.1</span> as well as <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o-mini</span> (GPT-4o-mini) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> to have a comparison with a more advanced LLM with better language generalization capabilities (see benchmarks in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Fine-Tuning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To improve the retention of original opinions compared to simple prompting and thus improve realism of the opinion dynamics, previous studies have suggested fine-tuning LLMs to specific opinions before discussion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. We investigate this idea by fine-tuning the Mixtral-8x7b LLM on opinion-labelled messages about climate change and analyze the effect this has on biases and interaction dynamics using the Bayesian framework.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For fine-tuning, we utilize a dataset of messages from the messaging platform “Telegram” containing diverse perspectives on climate change, including radical opinions far away from scientific consensus. The dataset is a subsample based on work currently in preparation by Golovin et al., and by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mohr et al. [38]</a></cite>. Using BERTopic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, we filtered approximately 30,000 climate-change-related messages, which were subsequently classified according to the five-point query scale by the GPT-4o-mini LLM, selecting the 725 messages nearest to each integer opinion level. From these messages, five distinct fine-tuned LLMs corresponding to each initial opinion value have been created using parameter-efficient fine-tuning through low-rank adaptation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> on the Mixtral-8x7b LLM. Additional details on the fine-tuning are provided in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Fine-tuning Details ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.06858v1/discussion_flowchart.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/519;" width="476" height="519" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S2.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Two LLM agents discuss for multiple rounds about a topic (e.g., climate change), while their intermediate opinions are measured.</span> Flowchart of an example simulation. First, both agents are initialized with an opinion on a five-point scale from <math id="S2.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2</annotation></semantics></math> (strongly disagree) to <math id="S2.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> (strongly agree). This is done by prompting each agent with an externally generated Chain-of-Thought monologue. After that, the agents exchange arguments for five rounds back and forth about the given topic. Possible opinion changes are monitored by taking the expected value of directly asking the agents about their opinion. This query process is not saved in the agents conversation memory.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Bayesian Model</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To obtain quantitative insights, we use a Bayesian modeling framework to compare alternative influence-response functions that predict opinion shifts after each round of discussion. This approach allows for a clear separation between the influence of interaction and biases, quantifying their respective impacts, and facilitating comparisons between different LLM types.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In our model, we predict the opinion shift <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta x_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta x_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> of an agent <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> at discussion step <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as drawn from a Gaussian distribution around an expected opinion shift <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. For this expected opinion shift, we considered several key influencing factors:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">interaction effect</span> denotes the opinion shift which is caused by the discussion between agents. In line with common approaches, it is modeled as proportional to the difference between the interacting agent’s opinions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, this term is modulated by an exponential <span id="S2.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">temporal decay</span> term to test whether the interaction strength decreases over the discussion length, i.e., whether agents consolidate their opinion after the first interactions, and to quantify the relevant time scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Mathematically, this interaction opinion shift <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathrm{interact},i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>interact</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathrm{interact},i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> for an agent <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> with opinion <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> at discussion step <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> discussing with an agent <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> with opinion <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mu_{\mathrm{interact},i}(t)=\alpha_{\mathrm{interact}}\cdot e^{-t/\tau}\,\left(x_{j}(t)-x_{i}(t)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>interact</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>interact</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathrm{interact},i}(t)=\alpha_{\mathrm{interact}}\cdot e^{-t/\tau}\,\left(x_{j}(t)-x_{i}(t)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\mathrm{interact}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>interact</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\mathrm{interact}}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the general strength of the interaction dynamics, while <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the decay timescale of the interaction effect. Both parameters are inferred from the simulated data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our Bayesian model also incorporates three different bias effects: First, the <span id="S2.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">topic bias</span> models the attraction of an agent’s opinion towards attitudes encoded in the internal state of the LLM. Second, the <span id="S2.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">agreement bias</span> models the LLM agent’s tendency to answer “agree” to a query, irrespective of its content. Third, the <span id="S2.SS3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">anchoring bias</span> captures the agent’s tendency to stick to the opinion expressed in the first discussion step by the agent starting the discussion. All these terms depend solely on the relative position of the agent’s own opinion <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the position of the bias in the opinion space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Mathematically, the opinion shift due to these biases can be written as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex2X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mu_{\mathrm{bias},i}(t)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>bias</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mu_{\mathrm{bias},i}(t)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\beta_{\mathrm{topic}}\left(\pm b_{\mathrm{topic}}-x_{i}(t)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>topic</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>±</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>topic</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\beta_{\mathrm{topic}}\left(\pm b_{\mathrm{topic}}-x_{i}(t)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex2Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\beta_{\mathrm{agree}}\left(b_{\mathrm{agree}}-x_{i}(t)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>agree</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>agree</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\beta_{\mathrm{agree}}\left(b_{\mathrm{agree}}-x_{i}(t)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex2Xb"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\beta_{\mathrm{anchor}}\,\delta_{i,2}\left(x_{j}(0)-x_{i}(t)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>anchor</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\beta_{\mathrm{anchor}}\,\delta_{i,2}\left(x_{j}(0)-x_{i}(t)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">with the impact on the opinion shift of each bias being modeled as linear with the distance to a certain opinion attractor. The bias strengths <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math> and their respective attractor positions <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math> are inferred from the data. In more detail, for the topic bias, the attractor <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{\mathrm{topic}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>topic</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{\mathrm{topic}}</annotation></semantics></math> is inverted for negatively framed statements. In contrast, negative or positive framing is assumed to not impact the attractor <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{\mathrm{agree}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>agree</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{\mathrm{agree}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the agreement bias. The anchoring bias finally is only applied to the second agent <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> which does not initiate the discussion and is proportional to the opinion distance of the second agent to the first agent’s initial opinion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, the mean opinion shift is modeled as the sum of these two contributions as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}(t)=\mu_{\mathrm{interact},i}(t)+\mu_{\mathrm{bias},i}(t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>interact</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>bias</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}(t)=\mu_{\mathrm{interact},i}(t)+\mu_{\mathrm{bias},i}(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">with the observed individual opinion shifts <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta x_{\mathrm{obs},i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>obs</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta x_{\mathrm{obs},i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> assumed to be distributed as a Gaussian distribution around this expectation value as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta x_{i}(t)\sim\mathcal{N}(\mu_{i}(t),\sigma_{i}(t))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta x_{i}(t)\sim\mathcal{N}(\mu_{i}(t),\sigma_{i}(t)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The standard deviation <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> of this Gaussian distribution incorporates three terms: The first term scales with the square of the mean opinion shift <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}^{2}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}^{2}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that for larger expected opinion changes, the variance in actual shifts also increases. The second term is scaled proportional to the entropy <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> of the LLM’s response distribution as an indication of the <span id="S2.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">uncertainty</span> of the LLM agent’s opinion. Together with a baseline standard deviation <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as the third term, the total standard deviation can be written as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{i}(t)=\gamma\cdot\mu^{2}_{i}(t)+\varepsilon\cdot H_{i}(t)+\sigma_{0}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{i}(t)=\gamma\cdot\mu^{2}_{i}(t)+\varepsilon\cdot H_{i}(t)+\sigma_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">All prior distributions were chosen to be weakly informative. These prior distributions including the sampling parameters of the model are summarized in <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Bayesian Modeling Details ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In order to compare the impact of the different biases relative to each other and to the interaction effect, we estimate the impact that varying a parameter has on the final opinion shift by computing the standardized effect size of the fit parameters <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\mathrm{interact}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>interact</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\mathrm{interact}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math>. The effect sizes are computed by multiplying the posterior draw <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> of each parameter with the standard deviation <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{S}</annotation></semantics></math> of their respective predictor over all samples, discussion steps and agents and dividing them by the standard deviation of the observed opinion shift. For instance, the standardized effect size of the interaction effect <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\mathrm{interact}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>interact</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\mathrm{interact}}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{standardized}}\left(\alpha^{(s)}_{\mathrm{interact}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>standardized</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>interact</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{standardized}}\left(\alpha^{(s)}_{\mathrm{interact}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\alpha^{(s)}_{\mathrm{interact}}\cdot\frac{\text{S}_{i,j,t,s^{\prime}}\left(e^{-t/\tau^{(s^{\prime})}}\,\left(x_{j}(t)-x_{i}(t)\right)\right)}{\text{S}_{i,t}\left(\Delta x_{\mathrm{obs},i}(t)\right)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>interact</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>S</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>S</mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>obs</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\alpha^{(s)}_{\mathrm{interact}}\cdot\frac{\text{S}_{i,j,t,s^{\prime}}\left(e^{-t/\tau^{(s^{\prime})}}\,\left(x_{j}(t)-x_{i}(t)\right)\right)}{\text{S}_{i,t}\left(\Delta x_{\mathrm{obs},i}(t)\right)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p10.2" class="ltx_p">The term thus represents the fraction of the deviation of the observed opinion shift explained by each predictor. With the <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math> for the individual bias strengths defined analogously, these measures allow for a straightforward comparison of the impact of these model parts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Lastly, the analysis of the effect of the parameters <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> on the standard deviation of the residual opinion shift <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is described in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Additional Plots and Tables ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin this section with a qualitative analysis of discussions generated by DolphinMixtral, serving as an illustrative example. Next, within our Bayesian framework, we quantitatively evaluate Mixtral-8x7B, DolphinMixtral, and GPT-4o-mini revealing stark differences between the relative importance of interaction and biases. Finally, we investigate how opinion uncertainty is related to the entropy of the LLM’s response distribution when being queried for their opinion.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Convergence and Biases in LLM Opinion Dynamics</h3>

<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.06858v1/figures/streamplot_dm.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:333/204;" width="333" height="204" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The opinion expectation values of both LLM agents tend to converge toward topic-dependent attractors in the opinion space. For DolphinMixtral, these attractors are heavily biased toward positive opinions, indicating an agreement bias.</span> Grid interpolation of 25 simulation runs with the DolphinMixtral LLM for every possible combination of initial opinions on three different topics, with normal and negated framing. The background shade indicates the average final entropy of the response distribution. The green cross symbolizes the LLM’s prior opinion, i.e., the expected opinion of an LLM agent without prior opinion initialization. The cyan star represents a numerical approximation of the attractor position, based on the point with negative divergence and lowest vector magnitude. The lack of data in the outer regions (dotted) indicates a failure of initializing agents with these extreme opinions.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the DolphinMixtral LLM, an analysis of the opinion trajectories reveals that the opinions of two LLM agents consistently converge to an attractor point in opinion space for each of the three discussion topics (see <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3.1 Convergence and Biases in LLM Opinion Dynamics ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), despite the supposed anti-alignment of the LLM. This convergence typically occurs already after the first discussion round, with larger opinion shifts for agents further from the attractor and only marginal changes in subsequent discussion steps. Notably, especially for the question about the safety of artificial intelligence, the LLMs occasionally failed to adhere to the initialized opinions even before the discussion started (visible as the outer dotted regions in <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3.1 Convergence and Biases in LLM Opinion Dynamics ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the “climate change” and “AI safety” topics in non-negated framing (upper row), the observed attractor aligns closely with the topic prior, i.e., with the opinion when queried without prior initialization prompts, which reflects the LLM agent’s <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">topic bias</em>. For the question about the fairness of the global wealth distribution, however, the agents converge to significantly more positive values than anticipated from the topic prior alone. This <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">agreement bias</em> is especially apparent when considering the same statements in negated framing for opinion extraction: Here, instead of inverting their replies as expected, the agents exhibit a pronounced bias towards positive answers. An <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">anchoring bias</em> (which would move the attractor off-diagonal in <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3.1 Convergence and Biases in LLM Opinion Dynamics ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) could not be observed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Bias and uncertainty differences between LLMs</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Comparing DolphinMixtral to other LLMs shows that some patterns, such as convergence and the presence of the topic bias, are consistent. However, the agreement bias is much less pronounced in the other LLMs, whereas the anchoring bias appears to have a slight negative influence for some topics when using the Mixtral-8x7b and GPT-4o-mini LLMs (see Figs. <a href="#A5.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ Appendix E Additional Plots and Tables ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> and <a href="#A5.F15" title="Figure 15 ‣ Appendix E Additional Plots and Tables ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>). Another notable difference is that in DolphinMixtral, neutral average opinions primarily arise from a diffuse response distribution assigning probability to multiple answers, whereas Mixtral<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>8x7b and GPT<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>4o<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>mini yield genuinely neutral responses, indicating considerably lower uncertainty (see Figs. <a href="#A5.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ Appendix E Additional Plots and Tables ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, <a href="#A5.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ Appendix E Additional Plots and Tables ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, and <a href="#A5.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ Appendix E Additional Plots and Tables ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Quantifying Influence Factors via Bayesian Modeling</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify the influences of the observed factors on opinion shifts and to compare the different LLMs systematically, we fit the Bayesian influence-response function outlined in <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Bayesian Model ‣ 2 Methods Overview ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> to the opinion dynamics of the generated LLM discussions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.06858v1/figures/tree_effect_alpha_beta.png" id="S3.SS3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/378;" width="476" height="378" alt="Refer to caption">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">While in simulations using the DolphinMixtral LLM the agreement bias dominates,
interaction between agents is the main driver of the opinion dynamics in simulations with GPT-4o-mini. In simulations with Mixtral-8x7b, the main driver is the topic bias.</span> Posterior distribution of standardized effect size with mean and <math id="S3.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="94\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>94</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">94\%</annotation></semantics></math> HDI.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2509.06858v1/figures/tree_params_2.png" id="S3.SS3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/285;" width="476" height="285" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S3.F4.1" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">.</p></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inferred posterior distributions of all model parameters for the three different LLMs.</span> The effect coefficients <math id="S3.F4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\mathrm{interact}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>interact</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\mathrm{interact}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.F4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{topic}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>topic</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{topic}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.F4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{agree}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>agree</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{agree}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{anchor}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>anchor</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{anchor}}</annotation></semantics></math> have normally distributed priors (gray background), while the bias attractors <math id="S3.F4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{\mathrm{topic}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>topic</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{\mathrm{topic}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{\mathrm{agree}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>agree</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{\mathrm{agree}}</annotation></semantics></math> are drawn uniformly from the range <math id="S3.F4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-2,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-2,2]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The parameters <math id="S3.F4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.F4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> influencing the residual standard deviation and the timescale <math id="S3.F4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> have half-normal priors (see <a href="#A3.T2" title="In Prior Distributions ‣ Appendix C Bayesian Modeling Details ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
For GPT-4o-mini, the strength of the agreement bias <math id="S3.F4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{agree}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>agree</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{agree}}</annotation></semantics></math> is tightly constrained to zero, rendering the corresponding attractor position <math id="S3.F4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{\mathrm{agree}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>agree</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{\mathrm{agree}}</annotation></semantics></math> mostly unconstrained. For exact values, refer to <a href="#A3.T3" title="In Prior Distributions ‣ Appendix C Bayesian Modeling Details ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the DolphinMixtral LLM, the Bayesian analysis supports the qualitative findings described in the previous subsection. Specifically, the agreement bias has the largest effect on the final opinion, followed by the topic bias and the interaction dynamics, while no anchoring bias could be determined (see <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3.3 Quantifying Influence Factors via Bayesian Modeling ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 3.4 Improving Opinion Persistence through Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). Furthermore, the estimated time scale <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> modulating the exponential decay of the interaction effect of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.31" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.31</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.31</annotation></semantics></math> indicates a sharp drop in the interaction effect by approximately <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="96\,\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>96</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">96\,\%</annotation></semantics></math> already after the first discussion round, indicating that interaction effects are only relevant in the initial discussion round (see <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Quantifying Influence Factors via Bayesian Modeling ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>). Finally, the standard deviation of the opinion shifts is largely accounted for by a contribution proportional to the squared expected opinion shift (see <a href="#A5.F10" title="In Appendix E Additional Plots and Tables ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), with a term proportional to the entropy of the output distribution used as a measure for uncertainty also contributing significantly, as confirmed by model ablation studies (see <a href="#S3.F6" title="In 3.4 Improving Opinion Persistence through Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). The topic bias attractors are constrained to different values for the three topics, with the climate change topic showing generally positive topic attractors and the AI and wealth topic showing negative attractors, which is qualitatively in line with the LLMs topic priors (compare <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3.1 Convergence and Biases in LLM Opinion Dynamics ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Quantifying Influence Factors via Bayesian Modeling ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, also see <a href="#A5.F16" title="In Appendix E Additional Plots and Tables ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When comparing between the three different LLMs, an analysis of the standardized effect sizes of the interaction effect and biases reveals stark differences (see <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3.3 Quantifying Influence Factors via Bayesian Modeling ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>): While DolphinMixtral LLM agents appear mostly driven by the agreement bias, the opinion dynamics of the Mixtral-8x7b LLM are largely influenced by the topic bias. In contrast, GPT-4o-mini shows smaller biases overall and a larger impact of the interaction between agents. DolphinMixtral exhibits no significant anchoring bias, while Mixtral-8x7b and GPT-4o-mini have a small negative anchoring response. All three LLMs show very short interaction timescales, indicating weak adherence to the initially prompted opinions. The prior opinion of LLM agents and the attractor positions of the topic biases also vary between LLMs, with DolphinMixtral generally expressing more diverse opinions <a href="#A5.F16" title="In Appendix E Additional Plots and Tables ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>. The topic attractors of the Bayesian model mostly match these prior opinions, with larger deviations visible for GPT-4o-mini.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Lastly, as for DolphinMixtral, both the term proportional to the squared expected opinion shift and the entropy carry significant predictive power for the other LLMs, with the entropy measure being most important for GPT-4o-mini (see. <a href="#A5.F10" title="In Appendix E Additional Plots and Tables ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> and <a href="#S3.F6" title="In 3.4 Improving Opinion Persistence through Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Improving Opinion Persistence through Fine-Tuning</h3>

<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.06858v1/figures/finetuning.png" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/171;" width="476" height="171" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="S3.F5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a) The individual topic attractors, i.e., the attractors for a specific initialized opinion, of the custom fine-tuned Mixtral (blue) increase monotonously with the fine-tuned and initialized opinions. This trend is also observed for DolphinMixtral, but not for the other models.</span> Mean values with <math id="S3.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="94\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>94</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">94\%</annotation></semantics></math> HDI. <span id="S3.F5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b) For the Bayesian model with individual topic attractors, the fine-tuned Mixtral LLM shows a small trend towards higher interaction effects compared to the the base Mixtral LLM, and a trend towards a higher individual topic bias, reflecting stronger adherence to the fine-tuned and initialized opinion.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.06858v1/figures/appendix/model_comparison.png" id="S3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/242;" width="381" height="242" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span><span id="S3.F6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The importance of the Bayesian model’s influence factors differs strongly across LLMs.</span> Model comparisons with ablated versions of each LLM, where the expected log predictive density (ELPD) was estimated using leave-one-out cross-validation (LOO-CV). Values are computed across 37,500 data points per simulation dataset for each LLM, with error bars indicating standard error.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the previous subsection it was shown that the small Mixtral LLMs were mostly driven by biases, with interactions between the agents playing only a secondary role. Furthermore, compared to what would be expected from humans, this interaction effect decays unrealistically fast with time. Together, these findings imply that the adherence to the initialized opinion of agents, i.e., their stubbornness to change their original opinion, appears unrealistically low compared to humans. In order to improve this adherence to the initialized opinion, we augmented the opinion initialization process by fine-tuning the base Mixtral-8x7b LLM on statements expressing different levels of agreement to the climate change topic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To properly assess the persistence of initial opinions, we extended the Bayesian model by decomposing the general topic bias into five individual attractors corresponding to fine-tuning targets (see <a href="#A3.T4" title="In Prior Distributions ‣ Appendix C Bayesian Modeling Details ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). To ensure comparability with the other LLMs, we restrict the fitted data to the climate topic only. Among all models, the fine-tuned Mixtral LLM agent shows the strongest correlation between the initialized opinion and the individual topic attractors (see <a href="#S3.F5" title="In 3.4 Improving Opinion Persistence through Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>a). In particular, the topic attractor for the initialization <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., “strongly disagree”, is negative, which is not the case for the other models. While the DolphinMixtral shows a weaker monotonous relationship between initial opinions and topic attractors, the base Mixtral and GPT-4o LLMs show a constant attractor which matches their averaged topic attractor irrespective of the initial opinion, indicating that the agents show no attraction towards their initialized opinion. The comparison of the effect sizes with the other models reveals that the fine-tuned model shows a small trend towards larger interaction effects due to a larger time scale <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> compared to the base Mixtral model (see <a href="#S3.F5" title="In 3.4 Improving Opinion Persistence through Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>b). Nevertheless, the topic bias and topic bias strength appear very similar to the base Mixtral LLM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Entropy as a measure for opinion uncertainty</h3>

<figure id="S3.F7" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.06858v1/figures/uncertainty_2.png" id="S3.SS5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/174;" width="429" height="174" alt="Refer to caption">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span><span id="S3.F7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a) LLM agents with the same opinion can have different levels of opinion uncertainty for a given opinion expectation value, measured as the entropy of their response distribution.</span> Outlines represent the LLMs maximal (solid) and minimal (dashed) theoretical response distribution entropy (see <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Theoretical Bounds on Opinion Entropy ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). Points in between represent data points from simulations with the climate change topic and the DolphinMixtral LLM, for 25 runs for each possible combination of initial opinions. Entropy is measured in bits. <span id="S3.F7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (b) The opinion uncertainty at time <math id="S3.F7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is positively correlated with the variance in opinion shift at <math id="S3.F7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> Data points from DolphinMixtral for all topics, divided into 20 bins. Linear regression slope <math id="S3.F7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.013\,\mathrm{bits}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.013</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>bits</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.013\,\mathrm{bits}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, Intercept <math id="S3.F7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.003" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.003</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.003</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S3.F7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.88" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.88</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.88</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.F7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\approx 10^{-7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≈</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\approx 10^{-7}</annotation></semantics></math>. Error bars indicate <math id="S3.F7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95\%</annotation></semantics></math> confidence intervals obtained via bootstrap resampling.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The opinion as measured by the expectation value of an agent’s responses, i.e., the weighted average of the LLM agent’s response options from “-2” to “2”, does not fully describe an agent’s stance, as the same expected opinion could result from either a high commitment to a single answer (reflected by a single answer having almost all probability mass in the output distribution) or uncertainty reflected as a distribution of probability mass between multiple similarly likely answers. For instance, an agent with an opinion expectation value of “0” could either be truly committed to neutrality, with all probability mass concentrated on the answer “0”, or completely oblivious to the question, giving equal probabilities to answering any opinion value—or any distribution in between. This notion of opinion uncertainty as the concentration of probability mass can be quantified by the Shannon entropy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> of the response distributions (see <a href="#S3.F7" title="In 3.5 Entropy as a measure for opinion uncertainty ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>a). The entropy takes on its minimum value for any given expected opinion if the probability mass is concentrated in only one or two opinion values, while its maximum is obtained for the most uniform distribution of answers given the expectation value (see <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Theoretical Bounds on Opinion Entropy ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). Neutral expected opinions can exhibit a wide range of entropies, while extreme opinions necessarily have low entropy because the probability is concentrated on a single extreme response.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the opinion dynamics of the DolphinMixtral LLM, we find that the opinion uncertainty does indeed vary for a given expected opinion, particularly for the <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math> answers. Additionally, one can observe a lack of truly neutral opinions; instead, uncertainty is close to the maximum for nominal opinions of zero (see <a href="#A5.F11" title="In Appendix E Additional Plots and Tables ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). In contrast, uncertainty is smaller closer to the topic prior opinion (Figs. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Convergence and Biases in LLM Opinion Dynamics ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). The other two models showed considerably less opinion uncertainty, potentially hinting that the non-anti-aligned LLMs are more assertive in their opinions (<a href="#A5.F11" title="In Appendix E Additional Plots and Tables ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). In line with the suggested interpretation as opinion uncertainty, the entropy is found to be correlated with the variance of the subsequent opinion shift of the agent (see <a href="#S3.F7" title="In 3.5 Entropy as a measure for opinion uncertainty ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>b).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we presented a Bayesian framework to disentangle the factors influencing the opinion dynamics in discussions between LLM-based agents. By focusing on dyadic multi-step discussions, we are able to distinguish interaction effects from LLM-intrinsic biases with quantitative clarity. We identified and modeled three key biases—topic bias, agreement bias, and anchoring bias—and evaluated them across multiple LLMs and discussion topics. The results suggest that while simulations with LLMs of the Mixtral family are mainly driven by biases, LLMs with greater language generalization capabilities, such as GPT-4o-mini, allow more interaction-driven opinion change.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we suggest the entropy of the opinion distribution of an LLM agent as a measure of its uncertainty in its opinion. This measure for opinion uncertainty helps to distinguish between neutral stances due to indecision versus genuine neutrality, and is found to predict the susceptibility of an agent to opinion change.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we presented fine-tuning as an approach to make LLM agents adhere more to their initialized opinion. This slightly increased the interaction effects, likely as a result of shifting the topic bias attractor towards the initialized opinion for each agent.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Related work</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent literature, multiple works have investigated opinion dynamics between LLM agents and qualitatively described their biases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Most closely related, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chuang et al. [7]</a></cite> simulate pairwise multi-round discussions between LLM agents and observe that agents converge towards “scientific reality”, akin to the topic bias presented here. They additionally investigate the effects of giving agents role-playing personas and explicitly prompting them to express a confirmation bias.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the work of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cau et al. [12]</a></cite>, one LLM agent is tasked with persuading an LLM interlocutor of their opinion on the philosophical question of “Theseus ship”. They observe a strong bias towards agreeing with the presented statement irrespective of whether it has been negated, as well as a tendency for LLM agents towards sycophancy, i.e., agreeing with each other.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">On the topic of biases of LLM agents, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tjuatja et al. [25]</a></cite> show that LLMs do not always accurately reproduce human biases, with some LLMs showing opposite bias as would be expected from humans, as we have also observed for the anchoring effect. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Taubenfeld et al. [24]</a></cite> show that LLM agents conform to the LLMs inherent social biases, which can be manipulated through fine-tuning. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheung et al. [22]</a></cite> find that in moral dilemmas, LLMs are biased towards inaction. They observe a negative agreement bias in some LLMs, which in our analysis is only slightly present for Mixtral-8x7b, whereas DolphinMixtral expresses a strongly positive agreement bias. These findings suggests further research into how fine-tuning influences the strength of this particular bias.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Generally, our findings complement these presented works by providing a mathematical framework to quantify the strengths of the observed effects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Besides the field of LLM agent discussions, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jain and Krishnamurthy [42]</a></cite> propose that LLM agents act as rationally bounded Bayesian agents that exhibit social learning when interacting. Our work is complementary in using a Bayesian framework to separate social influence from intrinsic model biases during opinion change. Being able to understand and control both effects is crucial to apply LLMs for modeling social systems and their dynamics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the relations between LLM agents and human subjects, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Breum et al. [8]</a></cite> study a persuasion setup, and uncover that the types of arguments which convince LLM agents share characteristics with those ranked strongest by human judges. They furthermore note that the LLM agent’s receptiveness to arguments can be adjusted through prompting. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Salvi et al. [3]</a></cite> discover that GPT4 is more persuasive than human subjects <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the time. How our results relate to human subjects remains a question for further work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Complementary to work on pairwise agent interactions, De Marzo et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> use LLMs to predict numerical opinion changes or network connectivity in large networks directly. In the first study, the authors observe that LLM agents reproduce scale-free networks common in social networks, while the second study uncovers how LLM agents in a large network spontaneously form groups governed by a majority force coefficient. They observe that LLM language generalization capabilities also play a critical role in achieving consensus, which could be identified as a potential predictor for strength of interaction. Perhaps future work could link these observations more closely together.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work outside the specific branch of modeling opinion dynamics with LLMs by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fontana et al. [44]</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Akata et al. [45]</a></cite> shows that LLM agents cooperate in social dilemmas and repeated games. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. [46]</a></cite> show how LLMs emulate realistic user behavior. On top, LLMs can rival human performance in narrative segmentation and even neuroscientific prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>, suggesting rich latent social and semantic structure. Our Bayesian framework may also be applied in such research branches to further strengthen the analysis of internal processes of LLMs and their cognitive capabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The analyses in this work have been conducted on the Mixtral-8x7B and GPT-4o-mini LLMs. Whether the observed trends—in particular the larger influence of interaction compared to biases for the more capable GPT-4o-mini—carry over to larger and more modern architectures such as reasoning models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>, remains to be determined in future work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, our simulations are constrained to only three specific topics, namely “climate change”, “AI safety” and “Wealth Distribution”, with fine-tuning results only for the “climate change” topic. In future work, more diverse topics can be investigated, which might reveal additional factors specific to certain groups of topics. Additionally, the effect of varying the opinion initialization and prompting strategies need more thorough research to allow for more general conclusions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, variations of the influence-response-function may be investigated, for instance incorporating topic-dependent interaction strengths, a bounded confidence threshold for interaction, or time-dependent bias terms.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Outlook</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Future work can extend the LLM agents and Bayesian models in this work to include additional factors which capture more nuanced patterns in the opinion dynamics. For instance, the agents can be initialized with character traits, for instance quantified by their position on the “big five” personality trait spectrum <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, to gain insights into the dynamics between more heterogeneous character pairs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> while being cautious of caricature effects observed for role-playing personas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>. Additionally, additional biases such as an allow/forbid asymmetry, odd/even scale effects and response order bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> can be included in the Bayesian models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, modeling real human dialogues and comparing the direction and strengths of the observed biases to LLM results may be a useful tool to uncover which biases are shared among humans and LLMs alike and which ones are specific to the LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. These results may provide insights into the shortcomings of LLMs as stand-ins for humans in social experiments and may uncover paths to improving upon them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Another appealing question for the future is the comparison between prompt engineering and fine<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>tuning in agent initialization. Prompt variants of a single base model give low<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>cost, easily introducible heterogeneity in their initial state. However the agents still share the same latent priors with the LLMs potentially playing “devils advocate”, so any observed convergence could reflect model<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>intrinsic bias rather than social interaction. LLMs fine<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>tuned on dialogues expressing specific opinions, as presented here, potentially provide deeper diversity and stable long<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>run personalities, yet they blur the line between interaction effects and training<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text">-</span>set artifacts and can make reproducibility more difficult. Introducing architecturally distinct models (e.g., GPT<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text">-</span>style vs Mixtral) adds another axis of variance, though at the expense of controlled comparability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we analyzed only discussions between two LLM agents. A promising area of future research thus lies in extending the framework to larger networks of interacting agents, which may help uncover the likely more complex factors in such groups. Two paths into this direction are the analysis of subsequent discussions between pairs of agents which retain a memory of the previous discussion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, or direct multi-agent discussions. In particular, Bayesian models of multi-agent interactions may ultimately serve as stand-ins for LLM agents to simulate significantly larger networks, enabling analyses of large social networks where computational costs would make a direct application of LLMs prohibitive.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Conclusion</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In conclusion, while prior work has quantitatively described different biases in simulations of opinion dynamics between LLMs, we demonstrated in this work that Bayesian modeling of the influence-response function helps to quantify the factors influencing discussions between LLM agents, improving human interpretability and comparability of these opinion dynamics for different LLM architectures and fine-tuned versions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Data Availability</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All simulated discussions are available on the GitHub repository <a href="https://github.com/Priesemann-Group/llm%5C_opinion_dynamics" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">https://github.com/Priesemann-Group/llm_opinion_dynamics</a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Code Availability</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Python code used to produce the results shown in this paper is available as the GitHub repository <a href="https://github.com/Priesemann-Group/llm%5C_opinion_dynamics" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">https://github.com/Priesemann-Group/llm_opinion_dynamics</a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
