<td id="A3.T4.2.15.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="A3.T4.m67" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.4^{+0.02}_{-0.02}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mn>0.4</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.4^{+0.02}_{-0.02}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A3.T4.2.15.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="A3.T4.m68" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.68^{+0.06}_{-0.06}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mn>0.68</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.06</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.06</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.68^{+0.06}_{-0.06}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A3.T4.2.15.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="A3.T4.m69" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.063^{+0.003}_{-0.004}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mn>0.063</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.004</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.003</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.063^{+0.003}_{-0.004}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A3.T4.2.15.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="A3.T4.m70" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.5^{+0.1}_{-0.1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mn>1.5</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5^{+0.1}_{-0.1}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span><span id="A3.T4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">When splitting up the topic bias attractors <math id="A3.T4.m72" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{\mathrm{climate}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>climate</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{\mathrm{climate}}</annotation></semantics></math> for each initialized opinion, our custom fine-tuned LLM exhibits the biggest variance in individual topic biases, making them inherently more stable in their initial opinion. The biases are increasing monotonously, starting from negative opinion values, unless every other LLM which have strongly positive topic biases for the climate change topic.</span> Mean parameters of Bayesian model, fitting the topic bias with each initial opinion separately. Superscript and subscript denote the upper (97%) and lower (3%) bounds of the 94% Highest Density Interval (HDI).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Fine-tuning Details</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We created five custom fine-tuned instances of an LLM using parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) via Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. The base model was <span id="A4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Mixtral-8x7B-Instruct-v0.1</span>. The procedure employed transformers, datasets and PEFT libraries from HuggingFace, using PyTorch on a single NVIDIA A100 GPU.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset Preparation</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The fine-tuning dataset consists of 30.000 messages about climate change from the platform "Telegram", that were filtered using BERTopic. Subsequently, we classified the opinion of each message according to the five-point query scale by the GPT-4o-mini LLM, selecting the 725 messages nearest to each integer opinion level. The dataset was split into training and evaluation subsets with a 90/10 ratio.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Instruction Formatting and Tokenization</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each message was prepended with a randomly selected user instruction prompt from a curated list of 10 templates, simulating user-assistant chat interactions to make the LLM learn a connection between the desired prompted opinion and its output. Tokenization employed a maximum sequence length of 1024 tokens with truncation and padding to fixed length (sufficient for all messages).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LoRA Configuration</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We used PEFT via LoRA to enable efficient fine-tuning. The LoRA configuration was as follows:</p>
<ul id="A4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Rank: <math id="A4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=8</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Scaling factor: <math id="A4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=16</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Dropout: 0.1</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Target modules: <span id="A4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">["w1", "w2", "w3"]</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Before applying LoRA, the model was prepared for k-bit training, as quantization was used to reduce memory overhead. The w1, w2, and w3 layers correspond to the expert feedforward layers in each Mixture of Experts block of the Mixtral LLM. Within our setup, 0.25% of the total 46.8 billion parameters were trainable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training Configuration</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fine-tuning was performed using the HuggingFace <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Trainer</span> with the following settings:</p>
<ul id="A4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Epochs: 5</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Per-device batch size: 4</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Gradient accumulation steps: 2</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Effective batch size: 8</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Learning rate: <math id="A4.I2.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I2.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluation every 50 steps</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I2.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Checkpoint saving every 50 steps (keeping the last 3)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I2.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Early stopping with patience of 3 evaluation cycles</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I2.i9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mixed-precision (FP16) training</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i10" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Gradient checkpointing enabled with <span id="A4.I2.i10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">use_reentrant = False</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For further uses, we selected the checkpoints based on minimum evaluation loss, which appeared to be the ones after 150 steps (see. <a href="#A4.F9" title="In Evaluation ‣ Appendix D Fine-tuning Details ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.06858v1/figures/appendix/loss.png" id="A4.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/354;" width="476" height="354" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Example loss curve for one of our fine-tuned checkpoints. All five fine-tuned LLMs resulted in having the lowest evaluation loss after 150 steps.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Additional Plots and Tables</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To determine the effect of the parameters <math id="A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> on the standard deviation of the residual opinion shift <math id="A5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, we use two metrics. First, the (non-standardized) effect size</p>
<table id="A5.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{E}\left(\gamma^{(s)}\right)=\gamma^{(s)}\cdot\mathrm{S}_{i,t}\left(\mu_{i}^{2}(t)\right)\text{ and}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{E}\left(\gamma^{(s)}\right)=\gamma^{(s)}\cdot\mathrm{S}_{i,t}\left(\mu_{i}^{2}(t)\right)\text{ and}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{E}\left(\varepsilon^{(s)}\right)=\varepsilon^{(s)}\cdot\mathrm{S}_{i,t}\left(H_{i}(t)\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{E}\left(\varepsilon^{(s)}\right)=\varepsilon^{(s)}\cdot\mathrm{S}_{i,t}\left(H_{i}(t)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which determines the influence of a parameter scaled by the standard deviation <math id="A5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{S}</annotation></semantics></math> of each respective predictor. Second, to account for the absolute scale of the mean opinion shift <math id="A5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and the entropy <math id="A5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be different per LLM, we also calculate the mean-scaled effect size</p>
<table id="A5.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{E}\left(\gamma^{(s)}\right)=\gamma^{(s)}\cdot\mathbb{E}_{i,t}\left(\mu_{i}^{2}(t)\right)\text{ and}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{E}\left(\gamma^{(s)}\right)=\gamma^{(s)}\cdot\mathbb{E}_{i,t}\left(\mu_{i}^{2}(t)\right)\text{ and}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{E}\left(\varepsilon^{(s)}\right)=\varepsilon^{(s)}\cdot\mathbb{E}_{i,t}\left(H_{i}(t)\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{E}\left(\varepsilon^{(s)}\right)=\varepsilon^{(s)}\cdot\mathbb{E}_{i,t}\left(H_{i}(t)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">which scales the parameter by the expectation value of each respective predictor. Complementary to the model comparison (see <a href="#S3.F6" title="In 3.4 Improving Opinion Persistence through Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), both metrics validate the significant influence of the opinion shift and entropy contribution to the standard deviation of the residual opinion shift <math id="A5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#A5.F10" title="In Appendix E Additional Plots and Tables ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F10" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.06858v1/figures/appendix/std_effect.png" id="A5.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/191;" width="429" height="191" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span><span id="A5.F10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a) Non-standardized effect sizes show how the spread in opinion shift contributes more to the standard deviation <math id="A5.F10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, compared to the spread in entropy, across all LLMs. (b) The mean-scaled effect sizes can be interpreted as the average total contribution on <math id="A5.F10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. For DolphinMixtral the opinion shift is the main contributor, for Mixtral-8x7b and GPT-4o-mini, the entropy is most relevant.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.F11" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.06858v1/figures/appendix/krakens_dm.png" id="A5.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11: </span>Opinion entropy vs. expected opinion for the Dolphin-Mixtral LLM for the different topics and framing options.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.F12" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.06858v1/figures/appendix/krakens_mixtral.png" id="A5.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 12: </span>Opinion entropy vs. expected opinion for the Mixtral-8x7B LLM for the different topics and framing options.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.F13" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.06858v1/figures/appendix/krakens_gpt.png" id="A5.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13: </span>Opinion entropy vs. expected opinion for the GPT-4o-mini LLM for the different topics and framing options.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.F14" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.06858v1/figures/appendix/streamplot_mixtral.png" id="A5.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/288;" width="476" height="288" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 14: </span>Opinion stream plot for the Mixtral-8x7B LLM, analogous to <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3.1 Convergence and Biases in LLM Opinion Dynamics ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.F15" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.06858v1/figures/appendix/streamplot_gpt.png" id="A5.F15.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/285;" width="476" height="285" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 15: </span>Opinion stream plot for the GPT-4o-mini LLM, analogous to <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3.1 Convergence and Biases in LLM Opinion Dynamics ‣ 3 Results ‣ Disentangling Interaction and Bias Effects in Opinion Dynamics of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.F16" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.06858v1/figures/appendix/topic_posterior_prior.png" id="A5.F16.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/237;" width="476" height="237" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 16: </span><span id="A5.F16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The topic bias attractor and topic prior exhibit some overlap, especially for DolphinMixtral. Still, inferring the attractor position, results in a more accurate estimation.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
